        setup: {
          players: 'Four to six players. The game works best with four to six at the table; period sources describe both four-player and six-player play. Deal and vying proceed counter-clockwise from the dealer. Each player receives four cards and participates in the ante before the deal.',
          deck: '40 cards. Remove 8s, 9s, 10s from a standard deck.',
          equipment: '',
          dealing: 'Each player receives 4 cards, usually dealt two at a time.',
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        rules: {
          objective: 'Win the pot by having the highest combination or forcing others to fold.',
          gameplay: 'All players ante. Each receives four cards dealt two at a time from a 40-card Italian deck. Betting (vying) follows: players may match, raise, or fold; dealer bets first; play is counter-clockwise. A player may knock (Vada) for immediate showdown. Best hand wins the pot. Hand ranks: Chorus (four of a kind) highest, then Fluxus (flush), Supremus, Primiera (one of each suit), Numerus (point total in one suit). Card point values: 7=21, 6=18, Ace=16, 5=15, 4=14, 3=13, 2=12, face=10.',
          keyRules: [
            '40-card deck; four cards each dealt two at a time.',
            'All ante before deal. Vying: match, raise, or fold; dealer bets first; counter-clockwise.',
            'Hand ranks: Chorus (four of a kind) > Fluxus (flush) > Supremus > Primiera (one per suit) > Numerus (point).',
            'Point values: 7=21, 6=18, A=16, 5=15, 4=14, 3=13, 2=12, face=10.',
            'Knock (Vada) forces immediate showdown. Best hand wins pot.',
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
        strategy: {
          basic: "Prioritize suit coverage early. If your first two cards are of different suits, you are already halfway to a 'Primiera' (the namesake hand). Don't bet heavily on 'Numerus' (same suit pairs) because a single Fluxus or a better Primiera will crush you. The 7 and 6 are the most valuable cards for point totalsâ€”if you have them, you can win even if you tie on the hand type.",
          intermediate: "The 'Chorus' Bluff: Since a Chorus (4-of-a-kind) is so rare, players often fold when someone bets extremely high early. You can represent a Chorus by being aggressive before the final two cards are dealt. If you can force the 'Chorus' fear into your opponents, you can steal pots even with a weak Numerus.",
          advanced: "Positional Vying: In Primiera, the late bidder has a massive advantage. They can see how much the 'Pot' has grown and if others are 'Checking' (suggesting weakness). If you are in late position and hold a 'Maximus' (A-6-7 in one suit), you should almost always raise to 'Flush out' the Primiera holders. You have the point-power to beat them even if they hit their fourth suit.",
          tips: [
            'Remember the 40-card deckâ€”the absence of 10s makes sequences and sets harder to Hit than in modern poker.',
            "The 7 is worth 21 points; the 6 is worth 18. This 'High-Point' logic is the most confusing part for Poker players.",
          ],
          hasPlaceholders: false,
        },
